# Serial full-suite baseline

Measured before changes to test execution or test cases, at commit
`fa7f82b18e625421e88dc5e3193a2b573f70ac08`. Run the same command on the
same machine and configuration for the later comparison:

```sh
/usr/bin/time -p npm test
```

| Run | Wall time | Result | Assertions |
| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |
| 2026-11-25 | 149.57 s | exit 0 | 7,694 passing |

The test reporter showed a duration of 2m 27.9s. It does not print a test
case total; counting lines formatted as case headings in the captured output
produced 972, which is an estimate rather than a reporter-provided total.
Only one run was permitted for this attempt, so this is not yet a repeated
baseline and provides no measure of variance. Repeat the command before
changing execution to complete the baseline.

Machine: Apple M1, 8 logical and 8 physical CPUs, 16 GiB memory; macOS
26.5.1 (25F80), arm64; Node v26.11.1, npm 12.2.3, Git 2.49.0. The
existing serial command was used with installed dependencies and no worker
configuration. Other machine load was not controlled.

The run successfully exercised local HTTP listeners. This environment did
not deny them; a prior sandboxed measurement described in the spec did.
The earlier 143.6 s figure was an instrumented bundle measurement and is
not directly comparable to this full-command wall time.

No test cases, test commands, or CI gate configuration were changed to
collect this measurement.
# Contributing

Thanks for helping improve Roast. Bug reports and focused pull requests are
welcome. For large features, new dependencies, inference protocols, or changes
to artifact formats, open an issue before writing the implementation so the
contract can be agreed on first.

## Development

Contributors need Node 22.18 or newer and Git, on macOS or Linux.
The CLI, tests, and build tooling are TypeScript.
Clone the repository, then:

```sh
npm install
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm test
```

`npm test` builds `dist/cli.js` (`npm run build`), then runs the non-live test
suite in isolated Node processes; it does not type-check, so run
`npm run typecheck` too. Set `ROAST_TEST_WORKERS` to a positive integer
to limit concurrency (the default is up to four workers).
`npm run test:one -- test/<area>/<file>.ts` runs one test module with
a private temporary bundle, so focused runs can execute concurrently.
To compare timings, run `/usr/bin/time -p npm test` repeatedly on the
same machine and configuration and record the worker limit and load.
Scripts under `scripts/` are TypeScript that Node
runs directly, so they use only erasable syntax (no enums, namespaces, or
parameter properties).

`npm run build` regenerates `roast.schema.json` from the config schema,
bundles the CLI into `dist/cli.js`, and writes
`dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt`. Commit a regenerated schema with the
change that caused it; CI fails when the committed file differs. After a
build, `npx roast` in this repository runs `dist/cli.js`, the same way it
resolves in a user's project. Build before using it: with no build, a
`roast` elsewhere on your `PATH` can answer instead.

Also run these before opening a pull request:

```sh
npm pack --dry-run
shellcheck src/gate/pre-commit.sh
```

`npm pack --dry-run` lists the files that npm would publish without
creating or publishing a tarball. Check that the bundle, notices, schema,
and license files are present.

Tests use local HTTP servers and temporary Git repositories; they must
not require real model credentials or send repository content to an
inference provider.

Opt-in live tests in `test/live/` run a minimal two-turn tool loop (one
exploration call, its result, then the submission on the wrap-up turn)
against a real endpoint with a small output ceiling. They send a fixed
probe prompt, never repository content, and skip unless their API key
variable is set. `npm test` never runs them, and neither does CI:

| Variable | Default |
| --- | --- |
| `ROAST_LIVE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | unset (skip) |
| `ROAST_LIVE_ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` | `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` |
| `ROAST_LIVE_ANTHROPIC_MODELS` | `claude-sonnet-5-5` (comma-separated) |
| `ROAST_LIVE_DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` | unset (skip) |
| `ROAST_LIVE_DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL` | `https://api.deepseek.com/v1` |
| `ROAST_LIVE_DEEPSEEK_MODEL` | `deepseek-v4-pro` |
| `ROAST_LIVE_DEEPSEEK_PROTOCOL` | `chat` (or `anthropic` with an `/anthropic/v1` base URL) |

```sh
ROAST_LIVE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=... npm run test:live
```

With a DeepSeek key, a second test makes one structured call with a
256-token output ceiling, which a thinking model spends on reasoning
before it calls a tool. It checks that the error names the `length`
finish and suggests raising `max_output_tokens`, and skips if the model
answers within the ceiling. Never commit keys or paste them into logs.

CI runs `npm audit --audit-level=high`.
Roast has no runtime `dependencies`: libraries it runs are
`devDependencies` bundled into `dist/cli.js`. A new bundled library
must use a license on the allowlist (see "Third-party notices" below)
and should be justified in the pull request.

Pull-request CI runs on GitHub-hosted runners with read-only repository
permissions and no inference or publishing credentials. The `claude-review`
and `claude-mention` workflows are the exception: they hold an Agent Gateway
inference credential and can comment on pull requests, but review only
non-draft PRs from branches in this repository and answer only commenters with
write access. Keep that boundary: do not run contributor-controlled code in
`pull_request_target` or a privileged `workflow_run` job. Pin external actions
to full commit SHAs. Do not bootstrap privileged jobs from pull-request
artifacts.

Keep changes narrow and include regression tests for behavior changes. Update
the README whenever a public flag, configuration field, credential variable,
artifact, data destination, or exit-code behavior changes. Adding, removing,
or retyping a project config field also means updating `roast.schema.json`.

Pull request titles need a conventional `type:` prefix (such as `feat:`,
`fix:`, or `docs:`), checked in CI.

## Releases

Roast is published to npm only, from a maintainer's machine; CI never
publishes. `@substrate-system/roast` is one package holding
`dist/cli.js`, its third-party notices, the config schema, and Roast's
license files.

```sh
npm version <major|minor|patch|x.y.z>
```

The npm lifecycle does the rest. `preversion` runs `npm run lint`; npm
writes the version into `package.json`; `version` updates the README
table of contents and `CHANGELOG.md` (through `auto-changelog`) and
stages them; npm commits and tags `v<x.y.z>`; `postversion` pushes the
commit and tag and runs `npm publish`, whose `prepublishOnly` runs
`npm run build`. If `prepublishOnly` fails
after the tag was pushed, fix it with a follow-up patch release rather
than re-tagging.

## Third-party notices

`npm run build` writes `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt` from the esbuild
metafile (`scripts/licenses.ts`): one entry per package that has code in
the bundle, with its declared license and the license files it ships.
The build fails, naming the package, when a bundled package has no
`license` field, no license file, or a license outside the allowlist
`Apache-2.0`, `BSD-2-Clause`, `BSD-3-Clause`, `ISC`, `MIT`, `MPL-2.0`,
`Unlicense`. Extending the allowlist needs maintainer approval. When
changing dependencies, read the generated notices; automated
classification is not a substitute for legal review.

Prefer tagged stable dependency releases. Explain any necessary
development pin or prerelease in its pull request and revisit it when a
stable release permits.

## Security

Do not report vulnerabilities in a public issue. Follow [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md)
instead.
# Roast

An adversarial, cross-model code reviewer installed as a project dependency
via npm. One model proposes defects in a Git diff and a second model checks
surviving candidates when verification runs.

## Language

### Review

**Finder**:
The role whose model reads the diff and proposes candidate findings.
_Avoid_: reviewer, author model

**Verifier**:
The role whose model checks candidate findings against the cited code. It
can confirm, refute, or report insufficient context for a finding.
_Avoid_: checker, judge

**Role**:
One of the two review positions, finder or verifier, each bound to one
inference profile.

**Inference profile**:
A named, complete unit of model, endpoint, protocol, output limit and
credential source.
_Avoid_: provider, backend

**Run record**:
The stored evidence of one review, kept inside the project that was reviewed.
It can contain sensitive reviewed content, prompts, arguments, and provider
responses; excluding resolved authentication material from dedicated metadata
does not make the recorded content credential-free.
_Avoid_: log, history

**Gate**:
The pre-commit hook that runs a quick review of staged content and fails open.
_Avoid_: pre-commit check, hook

### Configuration

**Project config**:
The `.roast.jsonc` file (JSONC) nearest the working directory, searched upward
no further than the Git toplevel. It is the single home for every non-secret
setting.
_Avoid_: repo config, local config, rc file

**Trusted project config**:
A project config whose security-relevant contents (non-default endpoints,
credential variable names, prompt-override files) the user has explicitly
approved for this clone. Roast does not send code or credentials anywhere a
project config names until it is trusted.
_Avoid_: allowed config, whitelisted config

**Credential**:
An API key. Credentials are the only settings that come from outside the
project config: from environment variables, or from a stored credential. A
project config may name the variable but never hold the value.
_Avoid_: secret, token

**Built-in provider**:
A provider Roast knows by name (OpenAI, Anthropic, OpenRouter): its
endpoint, the protocols it accepts there, its standard key variable, and
how to log in.
A selected profile on a built-in provider's endpoint with an accepted
protocol and its standard or implicit key variable requires no approval on
its own; other selected profiles and prompt overrides also matter.
_Avoid_: default provider, known provider

**Stored credential**:
A built-in provider's key kept in a credentials file, scoped to the project
(inside the Git directory) or to the user. It ranks below every environment
variable and is sent only to that provider's own endpoint (ADR-0002).
_Avoid_: saved key, cached token
